| --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(xy+y) \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3+3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+18)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+10=11.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/state.json" "file_sha256" "7f50c06d597587ef706912ea9b536f4809b023367657ba94056973d20089442d"} {"question" "q-331650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7aca546a0d56627f8183bfb2c9d1a3326c8353f0cfd18d4a316ca3ae787d559b"} {"question" "q-331650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/state.json" "file_sha256" "7f50c06d597587ef706912ea9b536f4809b023367657ba94056973d20089442d"} {"question" "q-331650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/state.json" "file_sha256" "7f50c06d597587ef706912ea9b536f4809b023367657ba94056973d20089442d"} {"question" "e-21277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy + y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21277.json" "file_sha256" "87eabcff6bb8ca4ba093e63d5f265f3b3d4791798e4fd1ac3ea0575105d89adf"} {"question" "e-21277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p}={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21277.json" "file_sha256" "87eabcff6bb8ca4ba093e63d5f265f3b3d4791798e4fd1ac3ea0575105d89adf"} {"question" "e-21560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = xy^2 - x^2, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21560.json" "file_sha256" "543a9d2b38f40c956bdfb456d69b4e1a61c4ed0d835a65859a9eda0609ec7cbb"} {"question" "e-21560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{7}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21560.json" "file_sha256" "543a9d2b38f40c956bdfb456d69b4e1a61c4ed0d835a65859a9eda0609ec7cbb"} {"question" "e-21274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = xy - y^2 + 2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21274.json" "file_sha256" "fe00a1f4b7b0bd6fa641894150a44893cae0c9deff407d69c5283f1c0befcbcc"} {"question" "e-21274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-{y}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1-{1}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}-{y}_{p}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =(1)(2)-(2)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21274.json" "file_sha256" "fe00a1f4b7b0bd6fa641894150a44893cae0c9deff407d69c5283f1c0befcbcc"} {"question" "e-21275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2y^2+xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21275.json" "file_sha256" "ac0e606e115266d1944311c4070b5d2de0995d1e423081a19cccc0237ec08973"} {"question" "e-21275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{p}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}+2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+54)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 28 = 29\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n| $2$ | $29$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{29}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044701/example-21275.json" "file_sha256" "ac0e606e115266d1944311c4070b5d2de0995d1e423081a19cccc0237ec08973"}] "usage" {"q-330778" [] "e-21274" [] "q-323980" [] "e-21560" [] "e-21275" [] "e-21277" [] "q-324062" []} "basis" 1664 "retractions" [[17592186167120 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x - 2y, y(1) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$, fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |"] [17592186167103 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x^{2} - y^{2}, y(0) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"completing-the-first-row-of-a-table-using-the-modified-euler-method\"></a>"] [17592186167104 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2xy, y(1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $$? |  |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186166447 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186166447 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy_{p} = y^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy + y^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1 + 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |"] [17592186166461 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy^2 - x^2, \\qquad y(0)=2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ |  |"] [17592186166461 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′})Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(0 + \\frac{7}{4}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{16}$ |"] [17592186166445 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy - y^2 + 2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186166445 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= xy - y^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 - 1^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 1 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= f(x_{new}, y_{p}) \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{p} - y_{p}^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= (1)(2) - (2)^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |"] [17592186166446 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x^2y^2+xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$."] [17592186166446 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{2}y^{2} + xy^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot 1^{2} + 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 2 = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new}^{2}y_{p}^{2} + x_{new}y_{p}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 + 54) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 28 = 29\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n| $2$ | $29$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{29}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164002 17592186164003 17592186164004 17592186164005 17592186164006] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164000 :answer-field/id] [17592186164000 :answer-field/key] [17592186164000 :answer-field/type] [17592186164001 :answer-field/correct] [1759218616400